india has accused china of violating  border agreements  as the armies of both countries engage  in a tense standoff  along a disputed himalayan border  speaking in parliament indian defense  minister rajnath singh  said china had unilaterally altered the  status quo  along the border he also assured members  that the army was well prepared  for any eventuality and contingency  plans were in place  to ensure india's sovereignty  we have informed china through  diplomatic and military channels that  such activities are an effort to change  the status quo unilaterally  and it has been made amply clear that  these efforts are not acceptable to us  under any conditions  this year's recent incidents of violent  conduct of chinese forces are a complete  violation of all mutually agreed norms  according to the current situation the  chinese side has mobilized its armed  forces and sent  ammunition on the lac and internal areas  china has meanwhile reiterated that a  recent agreement between top  officials of both sides should form the  basis for the easing of tensions  during the meeting in moscow the foreign  ministers of  china and india reached a positive  consensus on jointly easing and cooling  the border situation  and we will continue to maintain close  communication through military and  diplomatic channels to resolve issues on  the ground  we hope that india will earnestly  implement the consensus reached by the  foreign ministers of the two sides  meet china halfway and make concrete  efforts to ease the situation  is a journalist and strategic affairs  analyst who's been reporting on the  india china  border standoff since it began months  ago welcome  the foreign ministers of uh both sides  agreed last week in moscow the chinese  and indian troops would quote  disengage from their current standoff  points  is that plan already dead given that  both sides are actually reinforcing  their positions as we speak  well this has been the norm since the  start of this crisis to say one thing  and do another and the chinese from the  time they entered indian territory which  was uh  towards the early part of may  have been firstly saying that they have  not transgressed the line of control  they are in chinese territory  and secondary secondly calling for  disengagement  and withdrawal but not actually doing it  on the ground  now the indian side has had the time to  build up troops as well  uh along the border along the line of  actual control  and the same thing is happening there is  one set of  rhetoric that is going on and the other  is a far more aggressive and a far more  worrying  deployment and counter deployment that  is going on on the ground  that is bringing the troops into face  with each uh with each other  and setting the stage for violence so  that's a worrying proposition  you spoke about indian territory and the  lse or the line of actual control  now indian defense minister rajnath  singh said in parliament that there are  court  deferring perceptions of the line of  actual control which  is of course the line across which this  standoff is taking place the opposition  congress is saying that the phrase  deferring perceptions of the lac  legitimizes china's claim  does it oh well yes in a way  because uh a government in a country has  to be quite clear where its borders lie  and if the other country the country  with whom it has a disputed border  says that the border does not lie in  place x but lies in place y  uh then this country cannot start saying  deferring perceptions and so on and so  forth  uh each country has to be clear where  its border lies uh  in a way if you are if you're  rationalizing this uh  this issue of deferring perceptions  you're also rationalizing aggressive  action by the other side to capture the  border up to where  that country believes its border lies so  uh i think that there's a there's a bit  of uh  sort of excuse making that is going on  over here when they're talking about uh  deferring perceptions the fact is that  the chinese have entered into  a territory that lies on the indian side  of the indian perceptions  and from india's viewpoint there can be  only one set of perceptions  so to that extent this is a bit of an  excuse-making uh  sort of statement it sort of seeks to  justify  china coming into territory that india  claims  that india has always held and that  india has patrolled for years  and if i'm not wrong there are reports  that some 1 000 square kilometers of  territory  that india claims as its own is now  inaccessible to indian troops  is this something that the government is  actually concerned about  yes the government is very concerned  about this uh the loss of territory  especially in the south asian context  is a very emotive subject so  this figure itself this 1000 square  kilometers  has been arrived at by joining uh  several points of incursion so to that  extent it's a it's not a  really valid figure but the fact is that  china has at at least  four or five different points along the  line of actual control  ingressed into indian claimed territory  to a distance of  15 to 16 kilometers at the deepest and  uh two or three kilometers at the  shallow points  so uh all these things add up to the  loss of a significant amount of  territory  and that's a very emotive subject and  something that the government is very  careful in handling  final question to you sir do you think  china and india are now  in this for the long haul  oh well that that's hard to say uh i  would say that  both countries would not like to get  into a winter deployment  uh in an area where temperatures  routinely go down to minus 20 and minus  30 degrees centigrade  where there's a windchill factor where  high altitude causes  very serious limitations to how much a  human body can bear and do  so taking on a winter deployment which  is something that would not  appeal to the chinese or the indian side  so both have a very strong reason to  de-escalate  and we'll have to wait and see if they  do that over the next two months  we'll leave it there for the time being  but thank you so much for joining us
 

